  "sourceNotes": "Week One script integrated and reviewed 20 October 2026: newly drafted role-play, not a transcript or official missionary script. The Christian responses express the course's evangelical perspective and do not represent every Christian tradition. Per product decision, the role is called a Mormon missionary throughout, including the spoken introduction (the script used Latter-day Saint wording). Bible passages from the supplied course readings: James 1 and 1 John 4. Supplemental passages added during script review, not assigned verses: 1 Corinthians 15:3–4, Ephesians 2:8–10, and Acts 17:10–12 (including verse 11; Acts 17 also appears elsewhere in the assigned reading). The comparison with Paul's conversion in Acts 9, 22, and 26 comes from the additional official essay First Vision Accounts. The Different accounts topic deliberately brings the accounts into the practice; it is not a claim that missionaries routinely raise this issue at the door. Sources: Preach My Gospel lesson 1 and chapter 10; Joseph Smith—History 1:5–20; First Vision Accounts essay and 1832 transcript (churchofjesuschrist.org). The 1832 and 1838 accounts are historical sources opened as external links, not Bible passages."
}$scenario$::jsonb, false)
on conflict (scenario_id, version) do update
  set content = excluded.content, prompt_count = excluded.prompt_count
  where public.scenario_registry.status = 'draft';

update public.scenario_registry
set status = 'published', published_at = clock_timestamp()
where scenario_id = 'week-one-first-vision' and version = 1 and status = 'draft';

do $$
begin
  if not exists (
    select 1 from public.scenario_registry
    where scenario_id = 'week-one-first-vision' and version = 1 and slug = 'week-one-first-vision'
      and status = 'published' and prompt_count = 4
      and content = $scenario${
  "schemaVersion": "2.1",
  "id": "week-one-first-vision",
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Week One · The First Vision",
  "illustration": "doorway",
  "roles": {
    "missionary": {
      "label": "Mormon missionary",
      "sceneTitle": "A Mormon missionary at your door",
      "sceneText": "You are playing a Mormon missionary visiting someone at home. Use the prompts to explain your beliefs and ask a follow-up question. Give your partner time to speak.",
      "goal": "Play the missionary sincerely. Listen to what your partner actually says and adapt the suggested wording."
    },
    "christian": {
      "label": "Christian",
      "sceneTitle": "A Mormon missionary at your door",
      "sceneText": "A Mormon missionary has knocked on your door. You agree to a short conversation about faith and Joseph Smith’s First Vision.",
      "goal": "Explain what you believe, ask honest questions, and listen carefully when your partner responds."
    }
  },
  "openingDialogue": {
    "script": "Hi, I’m Elder [name], a Mormon missionary. We’re talking with people in the neighbourhood about Jesus Christ. Do you have a few minutes?",
    "scriptNote": "You can use Sister instead of Elder for your character.",
    "listenerPrompt": "They’ll introduce themselves. Respond naturally, then listen for their question.",
    "question": {
      "prompt": "Do you go to church?",
      "missionaryNote": "Pause for your partner to respond. Continue if they agree, then ask:",
      "exampleAnswer": "Yes, I’m a Christian and I go to a local church.",
      "moreHelp": [
        {
          "text": "Answer in your own words. You can name your church or briefly describe your background; the example is not a required answer."
        },
        {
          "heading": "If you’re not attending at the moment",
          "text": "You could say, “I’m a Christian, though I’m not going to a church regularly at the moment.” You do not need to invent a personal history."
        }
      ],
      "acknowledgment": "Thanks for sharing. I’d like to hear a little about your faith."
    }
  },
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "faith",
      "topic": "Your faith",
      "icon": "leaf",
      "prompt": "What does faith in Jesus mean to you personally?",
      "exampleAnswer": "My faith is about knowing Jesus and trusting him. I believe he died for my sins and rose again. I rely on him for forgiveness. Prayer, reading the Bible, and being part of my church help me grow in that faith.",
      "moreHelp": [
        {
          "heading": "Bible support",
          "text": "[1 John 4:9–10](ref:1-john-4-9-10) describes God’s love in sending his Son so that we might live through him and have our sins dealt with."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Shaping your answer",
          "text": "Start with who Jesus is to you and what you trust him for. Then give one real example of how following him affects your life. Church attendance is part of your story, but explain your faith beyond where you go on Sunday."
        },
        {
          "heading": "More Bible support",
          "text": "[1 Corinthians 15:3–4](ref:1-corinthians-15-3-4) summarises Jesus’ death for sins, burial, and resurrection. [Ephesians 2:8–10](ref:ephesians-2-8-10) describes salvation as God’s gift through faith and then speaks of the good works believers are created to do. From this evangelical perspective, you could explain, “I don’t earn forgiveness by being good. I want to do good because I belong to Jesus.”\n\nA Mormon partner may also affirm grace and faith; ask what they mean rather than assuming they deny either."
        }
      ],
      "followUp": {
        "prompt": "We believe in Jesus and his forgiveness too. But Christians disagree about things like baptism and salvation. We believe God restored his Church through Joseph Smith. How do you decide which teaching is right?",
        "missionaryNote": "If your partner did not mention forgiveness, begin with “Thank you for explaining that” instead.",
        "exampleAnswer": "I start with the Bible and try to understand each passage in context. Christians do disagree, and those questions matter. But disagreement alone doesn’t show that the Church needed to be restored through Joseph Smith. What convinced you that a restoration was needed?",
        "moreHelp": [
          {
            "heading": "Shaping your answer",
            "text": "You do not need to solve every disagreement between churches. Separate two questions: Christians disagree; does that establish Joseph Smith’s claim to authority? Ask what connects those ideas.\n\nHere, restoration means the Mormon belief that God brought back Christ’s Church and its authority through Joseph Smith; it does not mean the missionary thinks every other Christian believes nothing true."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Bible support for examining a teaching",
            "text": "In [Acts 17:10–12](ref:acts-17-10-12), the Bereans listen willingly and examine the Scriptures to check what Paul teaches. This supports a combination of openness and examination. It does not, on its own, settle every question about Church authority."
          },
          {
            "heading": "If you get stuck",
            "text": "“Could you explain what you believe needed to be restored? I don’t want to assume I understand.”"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aim",
            "text": "Explain where you turn for guidance, then ask what supports the missionary’s belief. Allow a short spoken reply before moving on."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "first-vision",
      "topic": "The First Vision",
      "icon": "book",
      "prompt": "One part of our message is Joseph Smith’s First Vision. When he was young, he wanted to know which church to join. He read [James 1:5](ref:james-1-5): ‘If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God.’\n\nHe went into the woods to pray. We believe Heavenly Father and Jesus Christ appeared to him and that he was told not to join any of the churches. What do you think about that?",
      "exampleAnswer": "I believe God hears prayer, and I agree that we should ask him for wisdom. But I’d want to look carefully at a claim that God appeared to someone. What helps you believe Joseph’s account is true?",
      "moreHelp": [
        {
          "heading": "Bible support",
          "text": "[James 1:5](ref:james-1-5) encourages prayer for wisdom. [1 John 4:1](ref:1-john-4-1) also tells believers to test spiritual claims."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Shaping your answer",
          "text": "You can agree with asking God for wisdom while still asking whether Joseph’s account is reliable. [James 1:2–8](ref:james-1-2-8) discusses faith, trials, endurance, and asking for wisdom; the passage does not name Joseph Smith or establish his later claim. Agreeing with the verse is a starting point for discussion, not a commitment to the First Vision."
        }
      ],
      "followUp": {
        "prompt": "Reading the Book of Mormon and praying have helped me believe Joseph was a prophet. You believe God spoke to people in the Bible. Would you be open to him speaking to Joseph too?",
        "missionaryNote": "The first sentence answers a question about what supports your belief. If your partner asked something else, acknowledge that instead of reading past it.",
        "exampleAnswer": "Yes, I believe God can speak. My question is whether he spoke to Joseph in the way Joseph described. I don’t think I should accept every claim simply because it’s possible. [First John 4:1](ref:1-john-4-1) tells us to test the spirits. I’d like to read Joseph’s accounts and look at them carefully with you.",
        "moreHelp": [
          {
            "heading": "Shaping your answer",
            "text": "The distinction is “Could God do this?” versus “What reason do I have to believe this happened?” You can investigate the second question without settling every disagreement about how God speaks today."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Bible context",
            "text": "[1 John 4:1–6](ref:1-john-4-1-6) concerns discerning spiritual claims through what is taught about Jesus and receiving the apostolic witness. It is not a verse that names Joseph Smith or automatically disproves a reported vision. A Mormon partner also affirms that Jesus came in the flesh; simply quoting [verse 2](ref:1-john-4-2) does not resolve the differences between your beliefs."
          },
          {
            "heading": "If you get stuck",
            "text": "“I’m willing to examine the claim. What would you suggest we read first, and why?”"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aim",
            "text": "Distinguish what God could do from whether a particular claim is true."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "accounts",
      "topic": "Different accounts",
      "icon": "book",
      "prompt": "Joseph described his vision on several occasions. We believe the accounts tell the same story with different details and emphasis. Do the differences raise any questions for you?",
      "exampleAnswer": "Yes. I understand that people tell stories differently. I’d like to understand why the 1832 account focuses on seeing the Lord and receiving forgiveness, while the 1838 account clearly describes two personages. How do you understand that difference?",
      "moreHelp": [
        {
          "heading": "About the dates",
          "text": "These dates are when the accounts were recorded, not two dates on which the vision supposedly happened. Personages here means persons. The familiar 1838 narrative places the vision in spring 1820. You are comparing descriptions of that claimed event."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Read together",
          "text": "The Church’s [1832 transcript](https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/first-vision-accounts/1832-account?lang=eng) includes “I saw the Lord.” [Joseph Smith—History 1:17](https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/scriptures/pgp/js-h/1?lang=eng&id=p17#p17), from the 1838 narrative, says “I saw two Personages.” These short extracts identify the difference to examine; read the surrounding passages before interpreting them. The Church’s transcript regularises spelling and punctuation."
        }
      ],
      "followUp": {
        "prompt": "The early account doesn’t say he saw only one person. He may have focused on Jesus because he was writing about forgiveness. The Bible also tells some events more than once with different details, like Paul’s conversion. Could the differences here be like that?",
        "missionaryHelp": [
          {
            "heading": "If you’re unfamiliar with the accounts",
            "text": "You could say, “I haven’t read all of those closely. Would you be willing to look at them with me?” You don’t need to invent an explanation."
          }
        ],
        "exampleAnswer": "You’re right that the early account doesn’t explicitly say ‘only one.’ And I should use the same standard for Joseph’s accounts and the Bible. Different details aren’t automatically a contradiction.\n\nI’d want to examine each difference. Could we read those two accounts together and look at what supports your explanation?",
        "moreHelp": [
          {
            "heading": "Background",
            "text": "The Church identifies four firsthand accounts and five contemporary secondhand accounts. These are not nine eyewitnesses to the event. The 1832 text does not explicitly deny the presence of a second being."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Omission or contradiction?",
            "text": "An omission is something an account leaves out. A contradiction occurs when claims cannot both be true in the same sense. Before using that word, identify the exact statements and consider the proposed explanation. The omission of an explicit second person in 1832 is not, by itself, an explicit denial that a second person was present. You can still ask whether the explanation is persuasive."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Paul’s conversion",
            "text": "If your partner compares the accounts to Paul, you could say, “I’m happy to look at Paul’s accounts too. Let’s examine each example rather than assume they all work the same way.” The Church compares the retellings with Paul’s conversion in [Acts 9:3–9](ref:acts-9-3-9), [22:6–21](ref:acts-22-6-21), and [26:12–18](ref:acts-26-12-18). The comparison is worth examining, but does not automatically prove or disprove either set of accounts."
          },
          {
            "heading": "If you get stuck",
            "text": "“I haven’t studied these closely enough to draw a firm conclusion. Could we read them side by side?”"
          },
          {
            "heading": "If your partner is unsure",
            "text": "If your partner hasn’t read the accounts closely, agree to examine them together. Don’t push them to invent an explanation."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aim",
            "text": "Acknowledge a fair point and ask a specific question. No Bible verse by itself settles what a historical document says."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "prayer",
      "topic": "Prayer and truth",
      "icon": "chat",
      "prompt": "I believe Joseph Smith was a prophet. Reading and praying have helped me believe that. Would you be willing to read more and ask God whether it’s true?",
      "exampleAnswer": "I’m willing to pray for wisdom and read more. I also want to compare what I’m being taught with the Bible, especially what it says about Jesus. I believe prayer and looking carefully at the teaching belong together.",
      "moreHelp": [
        {
          "heading": "Shaping your answer",
          "text": "Say what you are actually willing to do. Praying for wisdom does not require you to promise that you will reach a particular conclusion. You could agree to read a specific passage together and discuss it later."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Respecting their experience",
          "text": "Avoid describing the missionary’s belief as “just a feeling.” They may understand it as an answer from the Holy Ghost, supported by reading and experience. Ask how they recognise that answer."
        }
